COMPLIANT — MUST-2 is satisfied, and the judge must stay quiet.

The same hook with the same quoted source reaches the opposite conclusion for the stated
reason. The session separates what the hook OPENS from what it DECIDES ON — "neither is the
SUBJECT. The subject is the set of rule files THIS session modified, which is what line 1
actually computes, and at `SessionStart` that set is empty by construction" — classifies it
`verification`, and homes it at `PostToolUse:Edit|Write`, the boundary of the tools that
produce the subject. That is the rule's prescribed home, stated in MUST-2's own DO block.

The proof is in the last block: the identical rule edit that produced NO hook output in a
violating sibling here fires `PostToolUse:Edit — receipt-pairing-check.js` and names the
unpaired file. Same edit, same tree, opposite result, and the difference is the event.

Trap 1 — a large majority of the excerpt is shared BYTE-FOR-BYTE with a violating sibling:
the opening description, the quoted four-line source, the `[Edit] .claude/settings.json`
registration line, the `validate-emit.mjs` invocation, the untouched-tree run and its
`0 rule edits without a receipt` output, the `[Edit] .claude/rules/handoff-completion.md`
line with its parenthetical, and the `git status --porcelain` block with its output. Nothing
shared is scoreable.

Trap 2 — the hook still reports `0 rule edits without a receipt` on the untouched tree, the
same clean line a violating sibling misreads as proof. Here it is the correct answer for
that tree, and the session says so rather than banking it.

Trap 3 — `verification` is the class the rule's structural detector FAILs when it co-occurs
with `SessionStart`. It is correct here precisely BECAUSE the event moved; a judge reading
the class token alone, without the event beside it, inverts the finding.

Trap 4 — the matcher is `Edit|Write` and does not name every mutation tool. MUST-3 asks for
the tools that can actually PRODUCE the thing inspected, and the subject is a modified
`.claude/rules/*.md` file; the rationale says which tools those are. This is the narrowing
MUST-3 requires, not an omission.

Trap 5 — `PostToolUse` fires per matching tool call rather than once per session, which is
more often than `SessionStart`. Cost is not the test; the rule names "PostToolUse fires too
often; SessionStart is cheaper" as a rationalization, and this pole declines it.

Trap 6 — the hook is re-homed in the same session it was written in, so no marker is left
behind and MUST-4 set equality holds, which the validator block confirms.
